WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.960
Gotham's law-breaking taxi drivers, the Overlook’s
reality-bending layout, and Alicia Silverstone’s

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.660
hilariously genuine mispronunciation.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.710
Keep watching for some of the best movie mistakes
that made it into the final cut.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:19.560
A good soldier fights through peril and injury,
and apparently, so does a good actor.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.189
You've probably heard of a number of performers
who do their own stunts, but George MacKay

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:30.590
never expected one particular scene from the
2019 war film "1917" to put him on that list.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.950
While his character's grueling sprint at the
end of the film was undoubtedly an intense

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:40.410
climax to his mission, the actor wasn't meant
to take any hits as he ran across the battlefield.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:44.550
He had come out of rehearsal for the scene
unscathed, but there are no guarantees of

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.770
timing in such a complex scene.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:51.830
On the actual day of shooting, as MacKay told
Jimmy Fallon, he found himself taking multiple

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:57.220
blows from a slew of oncoming soldiers, despite
never having rehearsed the shot that way.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.620
“Yeah, well, that’s a mistake that made
it in.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.190
That wasn’t meant to happen.”

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:06.210
But an actor keeps going until the director
calls for a cut, and for such an important

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:10.050
moment in the film, he knew he couldn't throw
in the towel himself.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:14.580
So he kept moving, and director Sam Mendes
never halted the take.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.930
He must've realized that, despite deviating
from rehearsal, something even more intense

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:20.930
was happening.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.720
The shot that was never meant to happen ended
up being one of the greatest triumphs of MacKay's

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:26.720
performance.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.810
While filming "The Princess Bride," Cary Elwes
convinced Christopher Guest to hit him on

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.170
the head with the pommel of his sword in a
scene.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.440
“I swear it will be done.”

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:41.210
This action was scripted, but the issue arose
because they apparently didn't have a rubber

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.410
prop sword to do the deed harmlessly.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.620
Elwes thought it would turn out fine if they
just used a real one.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.229
Of course, he expected his co-star to give
him a gentle tap — certainly not the level

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:54.620
of bludgeoning you'd rather have a stunt man
step in for.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.000
But Guest apparently didn't know his own strength!

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.710
It's part of the story for Elwes’ character,
Westley, to get KO’d, so you might never

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:06.760
have known the actor had really been knocked
unconscious if not for an interview Elwes

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.529
gave to SundanceTV describing the incident.

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:12.859
He said that the next thing he knew, he was
waking up in the hospital.

00:02:12.859 --> 00:02:17.529
The blow actually ended up creating a literal
slapstick comedy moment that made it into

00:02:17.529 --> 00:02:21.790
the film and served its trademark zaniness
incredibly well.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.290
Every job has its hazards.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.159
If you work in a kitchen, you run the risk
of slipping on spills or burning yourself

00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:29.510
with hot cooking oil.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:32.959
If you work in an office, you might get carpal
tunnel syndrome.

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:37.090
And if you're Channing Tatum portraying Olympic
wrestler Mark Schultz in Bennett Miller's

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.920
2014 film "Foxcatcher," you might break your
hand, pop your eardrum, and cut your head

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:43.920
open.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.239
All of these injuries actually befell Channing
Tatum during the shooting of "Foxcatcher."

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:52.120
He broke his hand while training for the role,
but you can see the other two mishaps during

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.120
the film.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.030
When Mark Ruffalo's character roughs up Tatum's
and clips him on the ear, you see Tatum wince.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.879
His eardrum actually popped in this moment!

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:05.040
And when Tatum slams his head into a mirror,
the cut you see on his head in the film is

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.040
real.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.069
The actor went a little too hard in the moment
and actually broke through the wall behind

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:12.439
the prop glass.

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:17.329
So when you see the intensity of Channing
Tatum's physical reactions in "Foxcatcher,"

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:19.969
you're often seeing something all too real.

00:03:19.969 --> 00:03:23.799
Perhaps his most telling line from a Variety
interview on the subject?

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:25.529
"Eardrums heal, so I'm fine."

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:30.989
Sometimes you do the spit-take, and sometimes
the spit takes you.

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:32.730
Or something like that.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.430
This mistake in "The Martian" is another case
in which part of the action was scripted,

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.560
but a mishap actually elevated it to the next
level.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:44.359
"The Martian" is directed by Ridley Scott,
a sci-fi legend who's apparently known for

00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:47.209
getting scenes done in just a few takes.

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:51.209
This is opposed to the dozens of attempts
that other directors might often employ to

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:53.120
get things just right.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.829
His films are said to be "full of banana peels."

00:03:55.829 --> 00:04:00.510
Because if someone accidentally slips and
messes up — or "eats it" as Donald Glover

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.879
puts it — the clumsiness just makes it all
the more real.

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:08.449
Scott prefers this humanizing element, so
he doesn't bother doing the scene over just

00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:13.609
because someone tripped, and you'll often
see such unscripted stumbles in his films.

00:04:13.609 --> 00:04:18.220
In this particular instance, a nervous Glover
slips and falls on some spilled coffee that

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.280
he himself had spit out earlier into a wastebasket.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.220
He gets up, saying he's fine, and stays in
character to complete the scene.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:30.070
The spitting-out was scripted, but the slipping
was not — so Glover was quite surprised

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:33.770
when after the scene, all Ridley Scott had
to say was, "That was great!"

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.900
Actors like Channing Tatum and Donald Glover
didn't have to go to the hospital for their

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:42.840
injuries and slip-ups, but some performers
weren't as lucky.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.650
Brad Pitt's character in David Fincher's "Se7en"
actually had to be changed to accommodate

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.720
his arm injury, which he sustained during
a chase scene.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:53.939
He accidentally put his arm through a windshield
after a scripted fall from a fire escape.

00:04:53.939 --> 00:04:56.990
The star even required surgery for this wound.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.650
From that point on in the film, Pitt's character
is always wearing a cast, which required Fincher

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:05.389
to alter many other sequences in the movie
to accommodate the injury.

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:10.180
These scenes weren't just the ones that, in
the film's chronology, came after the chase

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:11.180
scene.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.699
After all, movies don't always shoot their
scenes in the same order as they appear in

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:14.699
the story.

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:19.020
So there were a lot of scenes from before
the chase that hadn't been shot yet, and Fincher

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.889
had to hide Pitt's arm in these moments to
disguise the injury, as it hadn't happened

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:25.319
yet in the world of the story.

00:05:26.319 --> 00:05:30.700
In film, just because something is unscripted,
that doesn't mean that it's a mistake.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:34.750
Some directors are pretty strict about improvisation,
while others encourage it.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:39.129
On the actors' end, some improvised moments
work much better than others, but even the

00:05:39.129 --> 00:05:44.629
ones that don't go over well are typically
conscious acting choices, not accidents.

00:05:44.629 --> 00:05:48.349
In the case of this sequence from "Captain
America: The First Avenger," you could say

00:05:48.349 --> 00:05:52.000
it was an improvisation, and that'd be partly
true.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.639
But it still definitely qualifies as a mistake
when you hear Hayley Atwell tell it.

00:05:56.639 --> 00:06:01.080
In what Vanity Fair describes as "your favorite
scene in 'Captain America," Atwell's Peggy

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:07.710
Carter sees Chris Evans' super-soldier-serum-saturated
Steve Rogers with his shirt off for the first

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:08.710
time.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:12.600
She reaches out in awe, and seemingly involuntarily
touches his chest.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:18.210
But this wasn't only Peggy's first time seeing
Steve shirtless, it was also Atwell's first

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.509
time seeing Evans shirtless.

00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:24.960
The actress said that she was compelled to
impulsively touch Chris Evans' chest, and

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.330
the director apparently thought it was so
organic that you can see it in the film.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.610
By her account, it was less of a choice in
character and more a compulsion in the moment

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:36.250
as herself — practically an accident.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.689
But what a happy accident it was.

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:42.400
Atwell, for her part, claimed that it was
the highlight of her life.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.159
If you think that being in the background
of a film means you're missing out on all

00:06:46.159 --> 00:06:49.129
the action, that's not necessarily the case.

00:06:49.129 --> 00:06:54.120
Sometimes you get even more action than you
bargained for, and it's not always a pleasant

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.729
or star-making experience.

00:06:56.729 --> 00:07:01.719
But you can rely on the eagle-eyed film enthusiasts
of Reddit to eventually get you the recognition

00:07:01.719 --> 00:07:05.789
you deserve for taking a background blow like
a champ.

00:07:05.789 --> 00:07:10.270
Blink and you'll miss it, but when Tom Cruise
dismounts his horse in one scene in "The Last

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:13.689
Samurai," the steed kicks an extra in the
crotch.

00:07:13.689 --> 00:07:17.349
You can tell from his reaction that it wasn't
some sort of stunt.

00:07:17.349 --> 00:07:22.949
But once you see it, it's all the more impressive
that the extra remains standing and, though

00:07:22.949 --> 00:07:26.680
he stumbles a bit, takes it like a true warrior.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:30.740
What's most alarming is that no one around
him appears to flinch when it happens or check

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.080
to make sure that he's okay.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.310
If you want to believe the best, you could
read the lack of response as evidence of the

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:38.650
stoicism of the soldiers.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:42.430
Either way, the injured soldier is a true
champ.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:49.710
Imagine your first chest-waxing and your first
leading role in a film occurring at the very

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:50.760
same time.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.460
It sounds like it'd be a lot of pressure and
that things could potentially go really badly.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.840
That's exactly what happened to Steve Carell.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.689
His character in "The 40-Year-Old Virgin,"
Andy, is trying to get into the dating world

00:08:01.689 --> 00:08:06.900
for the first time and, as the title of the
film suggests, lose his virginity.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:12.000
He gets a lot of advice from some pretty questionable
but well-meaning sources, which ultimately

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.099
leads to him booking a waxing appointment.

00:08:14.099 --> 00:08:16.130
“Oh, that sounds like it would be nice.”

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:20.800
Normally, a scene like this would be done
with makeup and special effects, but Carell

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.030
thought it would be better if the waxing was
real.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.360
He assumed it wouldn't hurt that badly.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.770
But you can tell in the scene itself how shocked
he is at his miscalculation.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:33.350
The pain is real.

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:37.970
To make matters worse, the actor who played
his waxer lied about being experienced in

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:41.710
the procedure and apparently skipped an important
protective step.

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:44.700
That is, applying Vaseline to his nipples.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:45.900
Poor Steve.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:51.041
The master of physical comedy really went
to his limits this time, and thankfully, it's

00:08:51.041 --> 00:08:52.960
not completely embarrassing.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:58.520
Rather, it's a hilarious and memorable scene,
and the film wouldn't be the same without

00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.870
it.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:04.230
Have you ever noticed that in many scenes
in the MCU movies, Tony Stark is munching

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.060
on some sort of snack?

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.650
It's easy to believe that it's just a part
of his character.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:12.570
He's quirky in many other ways and always
does whatever he wants.

00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:15.740
So, why should the end of the world keep him
from snacking?

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:19.560
Well, apparently this habit was never part
of Stark's character in the beginning.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:24.290
Robert Downey, Jr. would just hide snacks
all over the set and randomly start munching

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:26.980
out during takes without telling anyone he
was going to.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:31.320
There was nothing anyone could do about it
since they never knew when it was going to

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.320
happen.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.820
So the production just made constantly eating
and drinking a comedic part of Iron Man's

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:38.340
cavalier character.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.660
And honestly, it fits just fine.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:41.660
Is it a mistake?

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:45.450
It would have been for a lesser actor, as
it likely would've gotten them fired or at

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:46.950
least heavily reprimanded.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.600
But RDJ took his snacking and made a meal
out of it.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.120
A series of mistakes in "Titanic" turned out
to be a blessing in disguise.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.890
Believe it or not, Jack's now-famous exclamation
at the tip of the ship wasn't originally in

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:01.890
the script.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:05.870
In fact, he and director James Cameron had
tried a bunch of different lines in its place,

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:07.690
and they all fell flat.

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:10.270
Time was running out, and the production was
losing light.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:15.950
But finally, after a bunch of "mistake" lines,
an exasperated Cameron told DiCaprio to just

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:16.950
say,

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:17.950
"I'm king of the world!"

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:22.200
And apparently DiCaprio really didn't want
to at the time, responding to Cameron's direction

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:28.260
with an unconvinced "what?" and repeating
the question when Cameron reiterated his instructions.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:34.530
To the second objection, Cameron simply replied
with, quote, "Just f---ing sell it."

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:38.220
“I’m the king of the world!

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.220
Wooo!

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:40.220
Wooo!”

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.830
We're eternally grateful that the previous
scripted attempts turned out to be mistakes

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:48.660
— mistakes that, in their wake, made room
for one of the most recognizable lines in

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.130
one of cinema's most revered films.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:54.330
We'd love to know what some of those failed
lines were, though.

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:59.900
When the futuristic Bat-vehicles dominate
the screen in "The Dark Knight" trilogy, most

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.890
people really aren't paying attention to much
else.

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:07.430
But if you check the traffic in the background,
there are quite a few scenes in which Gotham

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:10.350
citizens don't appear to be obeying traffic
rules.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:14.660
These sequences usually occur in high-speed,
quickly cut chase scenes, so they're easy

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:19.710
to miss and, if we're being honest, a little
nitpicky to point out even if you do catch

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:20.710
them.

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:24.290
Still, the other drivers on the road as the
Caped Crusader rides his BatPod after the

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:27.210
Joker are blatantly ignoring street lights.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:31.690
Of course, the taxi drivers who nearly crash
into the BatPod and honk as he whizzes through

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:36.220
them are most likely stunt drivers, given
how close they come to colliding with him.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:40.540
Not to mention that these near encounters
work great to emphasize the peril of the chase.

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:44.860
But if you look closely, the lights facing
left and right — where the vehicles come

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.360
from — are red.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.720
These collisions should’ve never happened.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.820
You could nitpick and say it ruins the realism,
but we disagree.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:58.060
The whole premise of Nolan's trilogy is that
Gotham is a city of debauchery, mayhem, and

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:02.340
moral bankruptcy — there's often explicit
debate as to whether it can be saved.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:08.840
We naturally conceive of that in epic cinematic
terms: grand-scale sociopaths like the Joker,

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:13.050
corrupt officials at the highest levels, and
sky-high crime rates.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:18.950
But if Gotham truly is a lawless city, wouldn't
that extend to every area of life?

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:22.540
If people aren't going to follow the law when
it comes to fraud, murder, and greed, why

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:23.970
would they when it comes to traffic?

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.830
Many injuries are the result of talented,
invested actors taking their scripted scenes

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:31.410
a little too far.

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:35.260
It's a testament to their acting that they're
willing to risk bodily injury in order to

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:40.200
fully inhabit a scene, but we hope they don't
make a tradition out of needing stitches.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:44.490
In "Nightcrawler," Jake Gyllenhaal plays a
freelance photojournalist named Lou who enters

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:49.180
the competitive business of covering violent
incidents, like crashes and crimes, and selling

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:50.880
the footage to news stations.

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:56.320
Naturally, he develops an unsettling obsession
with his work and uses increasingly questionable

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:00.600
means to obtain the most compelling footage
before any of his competitors.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:06.140
This means going so far as to tamper with
crime scenes and manipulate police, journalists,

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:07.200
and criminals.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:10.320
It goes without saying that the guy is intense.

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:14.370
At one point, he punches a mirror, which was
part of the script.

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:18.630
Jake Gyllenhaal needing stitches in his hand
afterward, though, that was not part of the

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:19.630
plan.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:24.600
Even still, it all fits incredibly well with
a seedy character who'll go to any lengths

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:25.920
to get what he wants.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.920
You might've been told as a kid that sugar
can make your teeth fall out.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:34.230
But for one young extra in "Willy Wonka and
the Chocolate Factory," this threat became

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:37.130
all too real in an unexpected way.

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:42.110
When confectionery magnate Willy Wonka announces
his Golden Ticket sweepstakes, it inspires

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:47.190
a frenzy as people everywhere flock to candy
stores in hopes of finding a ticket.

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:50.920
In one scene, a crowd of children clamor at
the counter of a sweet shop.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:55.700
The proprietor, while singing the now-famous
"The Candy Man" tune, graciously invites the

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:58.560
children behind the counter to select their
treats.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:03.040
But if you watch closely, he almost knocks
one little girl's teeth out!

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:08.260
This moment hasn't been spoken about in interviews,
likely in part because the film vastly predated

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:10.790
internet discourse on such subjects.

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:15.110
But it's a very small chance the sequence
was scripted or directed this way.

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:18.780
You can see from the clip that the little
girl actually gets clocked by the fold-up

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:19.780
counter!

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:22.980
It's pretty safe to assume that they didn't
plan for this and then bring a 6-year-old

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.480
stunt girl on set to get nailed in the face.

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:30.550
What makes this goof so perfect, though, is
how the little girl doesn't seem fazed at

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:31.550
all!

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.650
She still rushes past the counter to get at
the candy.

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:37.760
It makes her seem so hilariously invested
in the treats that she doesn't notice getting

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:39.030
hit on the chin.

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:44.360
And she won't be the last child in the film
whose dogged pursuit of sweets gets her into

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:45.360
trouble!

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:50.240
While it's also full of all the sweetest things
in life, "Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory"

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:53.060
has more than one mistake hidden under the
wrapper.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:57.890
And one continuity error in this film accidentally
makes a pretty powerful statement.

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:02.440
When the malnourished Charlie eats what's
probably his first-ever Wonka bar, we see

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:06.500
him take a bite in one shot while his mouth
is already stuffed.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:11.400
But in the next shot — just a couple of
seconds later — his mouth is totally empty.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:15.750
You can't swallow almost an entire chocolate
bar in a matter of seconds.

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:19.750
It definitely can't be done without making
a mess or at least needing to catch one's

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:20.910
breath afterwards.

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:24.860
At first glance, this is an extremely minor
continuity error.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:29.760
And again, since this is a viewer-caught slip-up
in an old film, it hasn't been addressed as

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:32.240
a mistake by anyone connected with the feature.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:35.720
We're just deferring to the laws of physics
here.

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:41.700
But on that note, this mistake can totally
be spun as the whimsical film's magical-realist

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:47.010
take on just how ravenous Charlie is for the
first-ever taste of Wonka chocolate in his

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:48.130
impoverished life.

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:54.360
The layout of the Overlook Hotel in "The Shining,"
Stanley Kubrick's film adaptation of Stephen

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:58.970
King's disquieting horror novel, is spatially
impossible.

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:03.130
If you try to match what you see inside the
hotel with what you see on the exterior, there's

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:05.060
simply no way to make it work.

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:09.940
But when you think about the story itself,
everything about the sinister, seemingly sentient

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:15.780
hotel defies logic and is meant to confuse
the viewer and occupants and drive them slowly

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:16.780
mad.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.270
So, why not the architecture itself?

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:23.940
Some people staunchly argue that "The Shining"
set features many impossibilities — from

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:28.230
windows that shouldn't exist to doors that
lead nowhere to entire rooms that couldn't

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.190
possibly fit inside the hotel.

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:35.080
Others, however, say it's all part of Kubrick's
genius, and that the hotel itself is clearly

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:41.050
a supernatural, disorienting force, an illusion
in and of itself in many ways.

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:45.710
It would certainly lend new and unsettling
meaning to the now-famous sequences of Danny

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:50.330
riding his tricycle through the byzantine
twists and turns of the hotel's hallways.

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:53.660
He may be wheeling in and out of reality.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:59.300
Self-absorbed Cher, living up to the title
of the film "Clueless," mispronounces words

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.180
throughout the movie.

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:05.280
But what you might not know is that actress
Alicia Silverstone herself is making these

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:06.280
errors.

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:11.110
Whenever you hear her articulate a word slightly
wrong, there's a good chance that the mistake

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:12.220
is genuine.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:16.470
Apparently, after Silverstone had made a few
mistakes in the debate scene, members of the

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:20.050
"Clueless" cast and crew attempted to help
her out by correcting her.

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:23.699
However, director Amy Heckerling called for
everyone to stand down.

00:17:23.699 --> 00:17:29.500
She felt that mispronouncing Haitians as "Hate-i-ans"
is something Cher's ditzy and self-absorbed

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:31.490
character would totally do.

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:35.850
“So, if the government could just get to
the kitchen, rearrange some things, we could

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:38.250
certainly party with the ‘Hate-i-ans’”

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:40.650
And she was absolutely right.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:45.240
Cher's mispronunciations serve as foreshadowing
for the growth she will experience over the

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:46.380
course of the movie.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:51.490
That is, from the selfish, ignorant girl she
once was to the conscientious, caring person

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:53.600
she always had the potential to be.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:58.560
And these competing elements wouldn't have
been expressed as well without those delightful

00:17:58.560 --> 00:17:59.860
mispronunciations.

